From 021f1b6295fe984ec674590bc4b3796fd3f12584 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cyril Rohr <hey@cyrilrohr.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 10:09:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] solver: reuse imported cache remotes when exporting

When a remote cache export reaches a record that came from an imported
cache, ExportTo loads the result into the local cache (FromRemote, then
GetByBlob with several bolt commits per record) and resolves its remotes
again. runCacheExporters always sets a compression option, so this
happens for every imported record, although resolving a remote without
forced compression keeps the blobs it already has. On warm mode=max
exports, "preparing build cache for export" grows with the number of
imported records while no blob changes.

Reuse the imported remote unless the export forces a compression that
the remote does not have. Forced gzip and eStargz exports still load the
result: the two share a media type, so only the blob content tells them
apart. When it loaded the result, ExportTo also recorded the resolved
remote as a second result for the record; without forced compression it
has the same blobs, and item.addResult keeps only the first of two
results with the same creation time and layer count anyway.

Loading the result had two side effects that exports relied on:

- It turned the registry importer's
  containerd.io/distribution.source.ref annotation into the
  containerd.io/distribution.source.<host> annotation that the pusher
  uses for cross-repository mounts. Do that conversion when marshaling
  the cache instead, through contentutil.AddSourceAnnotation, moved out
  of getRemote so both share it. mode=max exports keep the same
  manifests; mode=min exports, which never loaded imported results, now
  carry that annotation too instead of the importer's internal one.
- For a local cache, it copied every imported layer through the client's
  session, so a blob missing from the imported cache dropped that record
  from the export. Imported layers now answer Info through the cache's
  store when it can tell whether a blob exists, as the local cache's
  session store can, and marshalRemote's existing Info check skips
  remotes whose blobs are missing. The store is asked at most once per
  blob per import: marshaling checks every layer of every chain, and
  each Info on the local cache is a round trip to the client. Registry
  caches can't tell and are unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Cyril Rohr <hey@cyrilrohr.com>
